{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Goblin Mirror is a 1992 fantasy novel by science fiction and fantasy author C. J. Cherryh.","title":"The Goblin Mirror"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was first published in a hardcover edition by Ballantine Books under its Del Rey Books imprint, and featured cover art by Cherryh's brother, David A. Cherry.","title":"The Goblin Mirror"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Carolyn Janice Cherry (born September 1, 1942), better known by the pen name C. J. Cherryh, is an American writer of speculative fiction.","title":"C. J. Cherryh"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" She has written more than 60 books since the mid-1970s, including the Hugo Award-winning novels "Downbelow Station" (1981) and "Cyteen" (1988), both set in her Alliance-Union universe.","title":"C. J. Cherryh"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" She is known for "world building," depicting fictional realms with great realism supported by vast research in history, language, psychology, and archeology.","title":"C. J. Cherryh"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Her series of fantasy novels set in the Alliance-Union universe, the Morgaine Stories, have sold in excess of 3 million copies.","title":"C. J. Cherryh"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The utopia and its derivative, the dystopia, are genres literature that explore social and political structures.","title":"Utopian and dystopian fiction"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Utopian fiction portrays a setting that agrees with the author's ethos, having various attributes of another reality intended to appeal to readers.","title":"Utopian and dystopian fiction"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Dystopian (or dystopic) fiction (sometimes combined with, but distinct from apocalyptic literature) is the opposite: the portrayal of a setting that completely disagrees with the author's ethos.","title":"Utopian and dystopian fiction"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Many novels combine both, often as a metaphor for the different directions humanity can take, depending on its choices, ending up with one of two possible futures.","title":"Utopian and dystopian fiction"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Both utopias and dystopias are commonly found in science fiction and other speculative fiction genres, and arguably are by definition a type of speculative fiction.","title":"Utopian and dystopian fiction"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Four-Star Spectacular was an anthology comic book series published by DC Comics in the mid-1970s.","title":"Four-Star Spectacular"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The series was edited by E. Nelson Bridwell and ran for six issues from March/April 1976 to January/February 1977.","title":"Four-Star Spectacular"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The books were in the "giant size" format and consisted mostly of superhero reprints, with some new material.","title":"Four-Star Spectacular"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" A total of four characters from DC's roster of superheroes appeared in each issue — hence the title.","title":"Four-Star Spectacular"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" (Half of the title's issues, however, only featured three stories.)","title":"Four-Star Spectacular"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Each issue featured a Superboy story, a Wonder Woman story, and at least one other story (usually a team-up story).","title":"Four-Star Spectacular"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" All issues featured cover art by DC artist Ernie Chua.","title":"Four-Star Spectacular"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Cliff Nielsen is a book illustrator and comic book artist.","title":"Cliff Nielsen"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The Internet Speculative Fiction Database credits him with cover art for about 500 book and magazine covers published since 1994 Nielsen is best known for his work on projects such as Star Wars, The X-Files, Chronicles of Narnia among many projects including advertising campaigns, designs, and magazines.","title":"Cliff Nielsen"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" His illustrations have been recognized for their excellence by the Society of Illustrators, Print, and Spectrum among others .","title":"Cliff Nielsen"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Feature articles focusing on his work appear in design publications and fanzine magazines.","title":"Cliff Nielsen"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Nielsen has been an international speaker on digital art and has served as a judge for the Society of Illustrators and a variety of professional illustration award programs.","title":"Cliff Nielsen"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" He lives in Los Angeles, California.","title":"Cliff Nielsen"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Leading Edge, formerly The Leading Edge Magazine of Science Fiction and Fantasy, is a semi-professional speculative fiction magazine first published in April 1981 and published at Brigham Young University in Provo, Utah.","title":"Leading Edge (magazine)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The magazine is known for its high quality fiction and has published stories by authors such as Dave Wolverton, M. Shayne Bell, Dan Wells, and Orson Scott Card, articles by Algis Budrys, as well as poetry and articles by poet and literary critic Michael R. Collings.","title":"Leading Edge (magazine)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Several former "Leading Edge" staff members (such as Brandon Sanderson) have become speculative fiction authors in their own right.","title":"Leading Edge (magazine)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Other notable former staff members include Anne Sowards, senior editor at Roc Books and Ace Books, and literary agent Michael Carr.","title":"Leading Edge (magazine)"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: C. J. Cherryh
