{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"A New Stereophonic Sound Spectacular is the first album by the Belgian band Hooverphonic when the band was only known as "Hoover".","title":"A New Stereophonic Sound Spectacular"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was the only album with singer Liesje Sadonius.","title":"A New Stereophonic Sound Spectacular"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The song "2 Wicky" was featured in the movies "Stealing Beauty" by Italian director Bernardo Bertolucci, "I Know What You Did Last Summer," and "Permanent Midnight".","title":"A New Stereophonic Sound Spectacular"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It is based on a loop from Isaac Hayes' cover of the Burt Bacharach and Hal David penned "Walk On By, and contains sample from Le Voile d'Orphée performed by Pierre Henry."","title":"A New Stereophonic Sound Spectacular"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The singles released from this album are "2 Wicky", "Wardrope", "Barabas" and "Inhaler".","title":"A New Stereophonic Sound Spectacular"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Killing Mr. Griffin is a 1978 suspense novel by Lois Duncan about a group of teenage students at a New Mexico high school who plan to kidnap their strict English teacher, Mr. Griffin.","title":"Killing Mr. Griffin"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Duncan developed the story from the character of Mark, who is involved in the kidnapping plan and is based on the first boyfriend of Duncan's oldest daughter.","title":"Killing Mr. Griffin"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Mr. Griffin was based on the personality of a teacher one of Duncan's daughters had in high school.","title":"Killing Mr. Griffin"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In 2010, the novel was reissued with changes to modernize the content.","title":"Killing Mr. Griffin"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"I Know What You Did Last Summer is a 1997 American slasher film written by Kevin Williamson and directed by Jim Gillespie.","title":"I Know What You Did Last Summer"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The film is loosely based on the 1973 novel of the same name by Lois Duncan.","title":"I Know What You Did Last Summer"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The film also draws inspiration from the urban legend known as The Hook.","title":"I Know What You Did Last Summer"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Who Killed My Daughter?","title":"Who Killed My Daughter?"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" is a 1992 non-fiction book by Lois Duncan detailing Duncan's search for answers in the unsolved murder of her eighteen-year-old daughter, Kaitlyn Arquette, in July 1989.","title":"Who Killed My Daughter?"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Locked in Time is a 1985 suspense novel by Lois Duncan.","title":"Locked in Time"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The story centers around Nore, a seventeen-year-old girl who moves into a new home during the summer with her father Chuck, stepmother Lisette, stepbrother Gabe, and stepsister Josie.","title":"Locked in Time"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Soon after she meets her stepmother, stepbrother, and stepsister for the first time, Nore begins to suspect something is not quite right about her stepfamily and later discovers they never age.","title":"Locked in Time"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Duncan says she developed the idea for the novel when one of her daughters was thirteen years old and was having issues with her body image.","title":"Locked in Time"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Duncan says her daughter was "taking everything out" on her, and she began to wonder what it would be like if her daughter never outgrew her adolescence.","title":"Locked in Time"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Love Song for Joyce is a 1958 début novel by Lois Duncan, under the pen name Lois Kerry.","title":"Love Song for Joyce"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The plot follows a young woman's struggle transitioning from adolescence to adulthood after moving from her hometown in Florida to attend a North Carolina college.","title":"Love Song for Joyce"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The plot appears to be semi-autobiographical, bearing similarities to Duncan's own life.","title":"Love Song for Joyce"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: I Know What You Did Last Summer
