{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Samantha Spiro (born 20 June 1968) is a double Olivier Award-winning English actress.","title":"Samantha Spiro"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" She is best known for portraying Barbara Windsor in the stage play "Cleo, Camping, Emmanuelle and Dick" and the television film "Cor, Blimey!","title":"Samantha Spiro"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":"", DI Vivien Friend in "", and Melessa Tarly in the HBO series "Game of Thrones".","title":"Samantha Spiro"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Cor, Blimey!","title":"Cor, Blimey!"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" is a 2000 TV film that follows the relationship between "Carry On" film actors Sid James (played by Geoffrey Hutchings) and Barbara Windsor (played by Samantha Spiro).","title":"Cor, Blimey!"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Carry On franchise primarily consists of a sequence of 31 low-budget British comedy motion pictures (1958–92), four Christmas specials, a television series of thirteen episodes, and three West End and provincial stage plays.","title":"Carry On (franchise)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The films' humour was in the British comic tradition of the music hall and bawdy seaside postcards. Producer Peter Rogers and director Gerald Thomas drew on a regular group of actors, the Carry On team, that included Sid James, Kenneth Williams, Charles Hawtrey, Joan Sims, Kenneth Connor, Peter Butterworth, Hattie Jacques, Terry Scott, Bernard Bresslaw, Barbara Windsor, Jack Douglas, and Jim Dale.","title":"Carry On (franchise)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Carry On Henry is the 21st in the series of "Carry On" films to be made and was released in 1971.","title":"Carry On Henry"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It tells a fictionalised story involving Sid James as Henry VIII, who chases after Barbara Windsor's character Bettina.","title":"Carry On Henry"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" James and Windsor feature alongside other regulars Kenneth Williams, Charles Hawtrey, Joan Sims, Terry Scott and Kenneth Connor.","title":"Carry On Henry"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" This was the first time that Williams and Connor appeared together since Carry On Cleo seven years previously.","title":"Carry On Henry"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The original alternative title was to be "Anne of a Thousand Lays", a pun on the Richard Burton film "Anne of the Thousand Days", and Sid James wears exactly the same cloak that Burton wore in that film.","title":"Carry On Henry"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Carry On Matron is the twenty-third in the series of "Carry On" films to be made.","title":"Carry On Matron"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was released in 1972.","title":"Carry On Matron"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It features series regulars Sid James, Kenneth Williams, Charles Hawtrey, Joan Sims, Hattie Jacques, Bernard Bresslaw, Barbara Windsor and Kenneth Connor.","title":"Carry On Matron"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" This was the last "Carry on..." film for Terry Scott after appearing in seven films.","title":"Carry On Matron"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" "Carry On Matron" was the second and last "Carry On..." for Kenneth Cope.","title":"Carry On Matron"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Carry On Again Doctor is the eighteenth in the series of "Carry On" films to be made.","title":"Carry On Again Doctor"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was released in 1969 and was the third to feature a medical theme.","title":"Carry On Again Doctor"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The film features series regulars Sid James, Kenneth Williams, Charles Hawtrey, Joan Sims, Barbara Windsor and Hattie Jacques.","title":"Carry On Again Doctor"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" This was Jim Dale's last "Carry On" appearance for 23 years until his return in "Carry On Columbus".","title":"Carry On Again Doctor"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Geoffrey Hutchings
